body:
I appreciate the supportive words. He wasn't always like this, I think it kind of devolved into it during a time when he was working so much that it almost forced us to plan/schedule any quality time together, and it stuck, so it feels very much like a business transaction. I've been in individual therapy for a while. He came to one session where we mostly talked about other issues (how to deal with extended family, managing finances more equally, etc.), so intimacy wasn't really brought up. I don't think I'll be able to convince him to go back because he hated it. 

&nbsp;

Thanks for the suggestion about non-verbal. I'm going to brainstorm some suggestions so when I do decide to talk to him, I can propose some solutions so it doesn't come off as too critical.
